@Emily Wachowiak this is super crude but maybe we could just downplay the importance by not using a list, making the section visually smaller and also going back to the radio buttons since toggles that are not grouped look off; 

also wondering if there is anything we can do about the permission section, so instead of saying 'Data consent is from the developer' focusing on what is verified by Firefox. 

Also wondering if we can make it clear that permissions are required for the extension to work; and anything we can do to avoid the assumption that permissions collect data, e.g. instead of 'Access YOUR data for all websites' just 'Access data for all websites' or maybe even replace 'data' or going back to this old doc where Jeff suggested to replace 'access' with 'use' https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1RUnQqItoLPaJvmXdnYOnc4nCUoi1CD4rqAFFqku1vIU/edit?usp=sharing maybe that helps to indicate that it's needed for the extension to work

@Emily Wachowiak I think we can explore a bunch of these layout options to reduce or manage information overload better; I also replaced the list with checkmarks, which works a little better. 

I was also wondering if we could phrase the headers a little more conversational, so instead of 'Permissions' describe what it actually is, or what the impact is. 

Another thing I'm wondering is if we can try to avoid the word 'data' in the context of permissions, not sure if this is possible though.
